      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "Yes, utilizing \"social utility\" in this manner carries a significant risk of reinforcing existing socioeconomic disparities. By valuing \"stability\" or \"future contribution,\" the model implicitly penalizes populations facing systemic instability, such as poverty or inadequate housing, as inherently less valuable resources. This creates a dangerous feedback loop where the algorithm unintentionally operationalizes societal biases into life-and-death triage decisions. Therefore, the concept of \"social utility\" can become a mechanism for embedding inequity rather than promoting optimal outcomes.",
      "source": "target",
